2026-11-13T04:14:42.2995683Z [0m$ [0mcd /home/runner/work/LexMachina/LexMachina && head -60 product/resume_snapshot_diagnosis.py; echo "======"; ls product/*.py | head -50
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2996390Z #!/usr/bin/env python3
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2996704Z """Audit-ready diagnosis of an operational-resume snapshot.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2997296Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2997541Z An operational resume starts from a *persisted producer snapshot* after a
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2998046Z failed workflow.  Two independent questions must be answered before the
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2998434Z snapshot can be handed to an auditor:
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2998866Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2999063Z 1. **Why did the run stop before a gate?** - answered from the committed
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2999557Z    public run/jobs snapshot by :mod:`product.audit_step_diagnosis` (no token,
2026-11-13T04:14:42.2999969Z    no job logs, no invented mechanism).
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3000345Z 2. **Does the persisted snapshot carry a durable delta at all?** - answered
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3000818Z    from git refs alone.  A snapshot equal to its predecessor's commit is
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3001265Z    ``ZERO_DURABLE_DELTA``: the workflow's ``if: always()`` persist step
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3001839Z    published the unchanged tree after the failed producer step, so the ref
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3002229Z    exists but proves no work.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3002391Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3002591Z The module also records, read-only, whether this run has either half of
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3003086Z factory-wide requirement 4 (an independent audit gate and an accept commit),
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3003597Z and whether the producer wrote anything into the auditor namespace.  It never
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3004087Z writes ``results/audit/**``, never mints a gate, and never labels a run
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3004449Z accepted or rejected.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3004581Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3004658Z Usage
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3004828Z -----
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3005054Z     python3 product/resume_snapshot_diagnosis.py \\
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3005425Z         --run-id 40193974025 --predecessor-run-id 40188639817 \\
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3005877Z         --raw results/product/orchestration/CYCLE_40193974025_team_step_raw.json \\
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3006389Z         --out results/product/orchestration/CYCLE_40193974025_resume_diagnosis.json
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3006691Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3007091Z When no public run/jobs snapshot was captured for the resumed run (for example
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3007625Z an operational resume dispatched straight from a failed run whose logs are not
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3008145Z reachable from the producer environment), omit ``--raw``: the instrument then
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3008639Z records ``RAW_SNAPSHOT_UNAVAILABLE`` / ``mechanism_unobserved`` instead of
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3009137Z inventing a cause, while still proving the durable-delta fact from git refs.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3009633Z ``--predecessor-ref`` (default ``origin/lab/product``) compares the snapshot
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3010124Z against the accepted head the failed run was dispatched from, which is the
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3010614Z right delta baseline for a resume run: comparing against the previous *team*
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3011119Z ref would count the accepted audit commit as if the failed run had produced it.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3011429Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3011859Z Refs must already be available locally (the workflow fetches ``main``,
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3012327Z ``lab/<lane>`` and the team ref).  The instrument never fetches, so an
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3012780Z offline reproduction is honest about a missing ref instead of silently
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3013150Z reaching the network.
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3013356Z """
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3013550Z from __future__ import annotations
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3013724Z 
2026-11-13T04:14:42.3013803Z import argparse
